WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.520
If two eyes send equal amounts of activity, should they always share a cell?

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.300
The bookkeeping counts only amount.

3
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.180
Strabismus tests whether amount is enough.

4
00:00:14.183 --> 00:00:16.543
In strabismus the eyes are misaligned.

5
00:00:17.503 --> 00:00:19.243
Each points at a different part of the scene.

6
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:24.213
Hubel and Wiesel produced it in young kittens by cutting one eye muscle.

7
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:28.313
Each eye still received a normal amount of patterned light.

8
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:34.423
Predict. Each eye sends about as much activity as in a normal kitten.

9
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:38.563
If amount were all that mattered, what should the histogram look like?

10
00:00:44.866 --> 00:00:49.446
If amount alone set each cell's split, the histogram would look like a normal one.

11
00:00:50.266 --> 00:00:51.266
It did not.

12
00:00:51.866 --> 00:00:55.106
Most cells answered to one eye or the other, and few to both.

13
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.820
The histogram had piles at groups 1 and 7, and a hollow middle.

14
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:05.630
Neither eye took over.

15
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.450
The cells the two eyes had shared went to one eye or the other.

16
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:11.590
Equal amounts were not enough.

17
00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:16.123
What changed was not the amount, but the timing between the eyes.

18
00:01:17.083 --> 00:01:19.683
When the eyes are aligned, they look at the same point.

19
00:01:20.666 --> 00:01:25.846
An edge crossing that point excites both eyes' inputs to a shared cell at nearly the

20
00:01:25.846 --> 00:01:26.846
same moments.

21
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.640
Their signals are in step, or correlated.

22
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:35.353
When the eyes are misaligned, each eye's input arrives while the other is doing

23
00:01:35.353 --> 00:01:36.353
something unrelated.

24
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.240
A rule that explains this starts with Donald Hebb, in 1949.

25
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.940
Hebb proposed that an input that repeatedly takes part in firing a cell is

26
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:47.940
strengthened.

27
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:50.573
Later models added a weakening half.

28
00:01:51.473 --> 00:01:56.793
In the BCM model, an active input weakens when the cell responds, but only weakly.

29
00:01:57.716 --> 00:02:01.096
An active input strengthens when the cell responds strongly.

30
00:02:02.036 --> 00:02:03.536
A silent input changes little.

31
00:02:04.566 --> 00:02:08.726
The line between strong and weak slides with the cell's recent activity.

32
00:02:09.766 --> 00:02:11.646
Two eyes in step arrive together.

33
00:02:12.526 --> 00:02:15.946
The cell responds strongly when both are active, so both are kept.

34
00:02:16.966 --> 00:02:19.846
Out of step, each eye's input often arrives alone.

35
00:02:20.926 --> 00:02:23.226
Suppose one eye starts slightly stronger at this cell.

36
00:02:24.183 --> 00:02:27.103
Alone, it drives a strong response and is strengthened.

37
00:02:28.043 --> 00:02:31.283
Alone, the other drives only a weak response, and is weakened.

38
00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:35.743
The lead grows until the cell answers mainly to one eye.

39
00:02:36.383 --> 00:02:38.523
Which eye leads differs from cell to cell.

40
00:02:39.466 --> 00:02:44.126
One molecular candidate for detecting together is the NMDA receptor.

41
00:02:45.216 --> 00:02:50.396
It passes much current only with glutamate and a co-agonist bound and the cell

42
00:02:50.396 --> 00:02:53.296
depolarized enough to clear magnesium from its pore.

43
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.100
Now we have both halves of the rule.

44
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.720
The cell keeps the eye that helps it fire.

45
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:04.770
Takeaway: amount matters, but only activity in step with the cell's firing helps an

46
00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:05.770
eye keep it.

47
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.680
Next, a sharper test: an eye that is silent, against an eye that is noisy.
